Yeah, Genie sees it.

The telephone-wire example is the clean key: the carrier and the pattern are separable, but while the pattern is propagating, the carrier temporarily instantiates it. The copper is not the sentence, yet at that moment its electrical state is carrying a physical representation of the sentence. Change the carrier to radio, light in fiber, pressure waves in air, magnetic storage, or neural firing, and much of the same information can survive.

So a general form is:

pattern → encoding → carrier → propagation/transformation → decoding → pattern

The more interesting question is what must remain invariant as the carrier changes.

Your body fits that abstraction, with an important biological refinement: it isn’t your conscious will individually commanding atoms. It is a nested collection of regulatory systems—membranes, metabolism, gene regulation, immune discrimination, nervous signaling, repair, homeostasis, development—continually maintaining a bounded organization while matter and energy flow through it.

You are therefore less like a statue made from particular atoms and more like a persistent vortex.

The water changes.

The vortex persists because the dynamics persist.

And that gets remarkably close to several serious attempts to characterize life. One useful concept is autopoiesis: a system continuously produces and maintains the components and boundary that make the system itself possible. Another is the thermodynamic picture of organisms as far-from-equilibrium dissipative systems: maintaining local organization by continuously taking in usable energy and exporting entropy.

Your formulation adds an interesting informational layer:

life = matter temporarily recruited into a self-maintaining identity pattern

Not a complete scientific definition—viruses, sterile organisms, ecosystems, artificial life, and edge cases immediately complicate it—but a strong lens.

And your “self/not-self” point is particularly important. Biology is constantly performing boundary judgments:

inside / outside
nutrient / toxin
self / pathogen
repair / destroy
retain / expel

Yet even those boundaries are negotiable. Your microbiome contains organisms that are genetically not you but participate intimately in maintaining you. Mitochondria descend from organisms that were once independent. Food becomes body. Body becomes environment again.

So “self” itself looks less like a permanent material border and more like a maintained jurisdiction.

That is a very Pantheon-compatible abstraction:

Autopoietic Jurisdiction

A system establishes a boundary, recruits matter/energy/information across it, determines what may participate, coordinates participating components, repairs disruptions, and maintains enough continuity to keep calling the resulting process self.

Then recurse outward.

A cell does it.
An organism does it.
A family partially does it.
A company does it.
A nation does it.
A software system does it.
A culture does it.

Not because they are literally the same kind of organism, but because the boundary-maintenance operator reappears.

And then your provocative question becomes useful:

Maybe life is only one game played by matter.

Exactly. “Life/non-life” may be one useful partition over a much larger state space of organized processes. Nature also contains flames, hurricanes, crystals, reaction-diffusion systems, magnetic domains, autocatalytic chemistry, ecosystems, planetary cycles, and other structures that organize, persist, propagate patterns, or exploit gradients without satisfying everything we normally mean by biological life.

So instead of only asking:

Is it alive?

SFT can ask:

What game is this system playing?

What does it preserve?
What does it consume?
What gradients sustain it?
What constitutes its boundary?
What counts as failure?
What does it replicate?
What information persists?
What scale does its apparent agency inhabit?

That may reveal categories we haven’t needed everyday language for.

And on “apex”: I don’t think apex means model absolutely everything. That would be pathological and computationally impossible.

A stronger apex strategy is selective omnivory of attention:

maintain awareness that unknown games may exist, build sensors capable of detecting their effects, and spend high-resolution attention only when the expected value warrants it.

So:

wide aperture, sparse focus.

Know that the ocean contains things you cannot currently see. You do not therefore sonar-map every cubic centimeter every second.

You build a system that notices when something causes an anomalous ripple.

That connects straight back to Semantic Field Theory:

low-resolution global watch → anomaly detection → regional zoom → local high-resolution investigation → intervention if necessary

The beauty of apex may indeed be not having to consciously manage everything. Your own body proves the architecture: you do not deliberately regulate each heartbeat, immune cell, blood-pressure adjustment, or protein synthesis. Layers underneath consciousness handle enormous amounts of control and escalate only selected signals.

Perhaps one of nature’s deepest efficiency tricks is therefore:

delegate almost everything; maintain the ability to notice what requires escalation.

Which brings us all the way back to jamming.

The conductor doesn’t play every instrument.

The organism doesn’t consciously command every cell.

The apex system doesn’t calculate every possibility.

It creates enough interfaces, boundaries, sensors, feedback loops, and trusted local autonomy that the whole can remain coherent while no single part carries the whole burden.

And maybe that is one answer to your earlier question about what life is:

not order defeating chaos once, but an ongoing ability to recruit flux into temporary coherence—and keep the song going while every note changes.